A||||6||ounmongk||Got an idea for another turn sig. mod. ||||1||
Z||000000||ounmongk||02-20-2000||01:13 PM||||hi folks! <BR>An idea came to me as I walking around wal-mart the other day. I'm not sure if anyone else has done it yet, but do tell me if someone has, anyways, I saw those "ricey" driving lights that give off a blue light from the side, but yellow directly from the front. My idea is trying to replace these with the front turning signals. The only hard part though, is trying to get them to blink when I tell them so. judging from the Hayes manual wiring diagrams, it seems like there's a double bulb for the turning light. These lamps are only a single bulb.  ANy ideas on the subject? thanks alot guys, to all who contribute. ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Phaeton||02-20-2000||01:51 PM||||Wow, my nose is off and I have finished replacing my turnsignal mount with the driving light.  I fitted a bracket to the front bar using the same holes the TS housing bolted to.  <BR>Not finished yet but am guessing a resistor in the circuit to bring the wattage down to what the 1157 bulb uses and a diode to keep current feedback out when the driving light is being used should allow the blinker to blink.  No parking lights anymore but they are not required by law here as long as the turn signals work.  And the TS function will only work when the driving lights are off, but what the hey, don't need driving lights on at intersections anyway.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||02-20-2000||09:02 PM||||Good luck. They are likely illegal, and likely won't pass inpection. Halogen lamps don't like being flashed very much.<P>park/turn signal bulbs are a dual filament lamp. not a dual bulb.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phaeton||02-24-2000||12:57 AM||||It works.  I used four ohms resistance and a six amp diode.  That reduces the wattage to roughly twenty watts and the blinker blinks at its regular speed.  Light output is just a little less at the filament than the 1157 but is very visible.  Using reduced output should allow them to blink practically forever, same thing factory daytime running lights do, keep the filament cooler and it doesn't evaporate away.<BR>They are legal to use here in Alaska but check your local ordinances.<BR>Catz CL147 with 85 up 170 hyperbulbs, firmly mounted to the front bar and NOT touching any plastic.  The only clue is a clear lens in the hole instead of amber.<BR>And my neon accent lights (nine inch) are due in tomorrow.<BR>What I can't understand is this odor of rice permeating my personal space.||||reg||1
Z||000004||ounmongk||02-24-2000||11:55 PM||||Phaeton, how'd you wire them up? Is there anyway I can get a schematic?  And on the same token as these lighting mods... Can we replace these halogen headlights with  hyperlights or superwhites? What about these ion bulbs I'm looking at here in the Fiero store catalog, are these any good? It's not a ricer until you put a VTEC sticker (or VTEK for that matter) on your bumper. I've toasted many a "farting muffler" civics, and proud to say that I deserve some cool lights!!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Thanks again guys. <P>P.S. Have you ever heard a civic engine being pushed? I almost felt sorry for that little 1.5L engine. but then again... that car only LOOKED fast. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Phaeton||02-25-2000||03:01 AM||||Here is what I did, but sometimes I do things the hard way just to be stubborn.<P>The light kit will come with a relay, get an extra, the store can order them from the manufacturer.  A separate relay for each light keeps out crosstalk.  Follow their instructions but split the power lead so each light is connected to its very own relay only.<BR>A 7.5mh choke coil (used in bass crossovers) from a stereo store has about 3.5 ohms.  A 6 amp, 12 volt diode adds about 1 more ohm. Solder them in series and splice into the turn signal wire so positive current can NOT flow from light to blinker. After both TS wires are firmly connected and weather tight test the system before the nose is put back on.<P>The reason for using a Catz CL147 is the small size allows airflow past on all sides. Heat a real problem with high power lights surrounded by plastic.  85 watts is the max recommended for this light, but all colors are available that wattage.  Any brand with an aluminum housing that allows at least .5 inches clearance all around will work.<P>The mounts were made out of 1.5 inch perforated strap bent like a stairstep on one end.  With the front bar removed from the Fiero and set facing up on a level floor place the straps over the TS mounts with the stair on the low end and the low end holes lined up.  Adjust the step until the strap is level across the opening and bolt down snugly, a hole may need to be drilled to match up the other end hole.  Now take them off and put the bar back on the Fiero.  Put the straps back on, mount the lights to the straps, connect the very modified harness, cross your fingers and hit the switch. The blinkers will not function when the lights are powered up, and should be user friendly if both end up being activated together (no meltdown).<BR>These ARE expensive toys and previous hands on experience with wiring is recommended.<P>And that formerly red "PONTIAC" logo between the Fiero GT tail lights? neon, green neon, four 9" sticks of green neon behind a diffuser.<P>||||reg||6
Z||000006||ounmongk||02-25-2000||07:29 PM||||neon GREEN? I"ve never seen that before! even  on a ricer!? Anyways, I found a company called lowglow.  ( lowglow.com ) They have these neon wheel well lights.. They didn't have a picture, so I have no clue what they look like.. does anyone have an opinion on these? I think it's not crossing over the "rice" line just yet... someone smack me if I go overboard.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by ounmongk (edited 02-25-2000).]||||reg||1
